{
  name: kmac
  import_testplans: ["hw/dv/tools/dvsim/testplans/csr_testplan.hjson",
                     "hw/dv/tools/dvsim/testplans/intr_test_testplan.hjson",
                     "hw/dv/tools/dvsim/testplans/mem_testplan.hjson",
                     "hw/dv/tools/dvsim/testplans/alert_test_testplan.hjson",
                     "hw/dv/tools/dvsim/testplans/shadow_reg_errors_testplan.hjson",
                     "hw/dv/tools/dvsim/testplans/stress_all_with_reset_testplan.hjson",
                     "hw/dv/tools/dvsim/testplans/tl_device_access_types_testplan.hjson",
                     "kmac_sec_cm_testplan.hjson"]
  testpoints: [
    {
      name: smoke
      desc: '''
            KMAC smoke test will contain a number of rounds, and acts as a base for many other tests.
            In each round, we run a full KMAC hashing operation:
              - Randomly enable interrupts, operation mode (SHA3/SHAKE/CSHAKE/KMAC), key length (if
                applicable), constraining all settings to be legal.
              - Set function name to "KMAC" and set customization string as empty if applicable.
              - Randomly set endianness of input msg and internal keccak state.
              - Randomly provide a sideloaded key, do not set cfg.sideload.
              - Set output length between 1-`keccak_rate` bytes if applicable.
              - Randomly select either SW or EDN as the source of entropy
              - Trigger KMAC to start absorbing input message.
                - During absorption stage randomly read from STATE window, expect 0.
              - Write message to MSG_FIFO window, maximum length of 32 bytes.
              - Check SHA3 engine status.
              - Trigger KMAC to finish absorbing stage, check kmac_done is set and status.squeeze is
                set.
              - Read output digest, and compare against output from reference C++ model.
                - In masked configuration: both shares are XORed to get unmasked result digest.
                - In unmasked configuration: Share1 is the result digest, Share2 should be 0.
              - Signal cmd.done to tell KMAC to clear internal state.
              - Try reading output digest again, confirm that it is 0.

              This test, and all other tests in the testplan, will be checked for correctness
              in two different ways:
              - first, a DPI-C model is used to check that the correct digest value is produced
              - a cycle-accurate model is implemented in the scoreboard to provide constant checks
                during each hash operation and ensure that internal state is being updated
                correctly
            '''
      stage: V1
      tests: ["{name}_smoke"]
    }
    {
      name: long_msg_and_output
      desc: '''
            Same as the smoke test, except with a message of up to 100KB.
            Max firmware input size for KMAC is around 392KB, but this is too large for a DV
            simulation.
            Average input size from firmware would be around 60-100KB, so we use 100KB as a max
            input size for DV, but will enable easy extensibility for emulation testing where we can
            enable much larger messages.
            Allow output length to vary up to 1KB (for XOF functions).
            If output length is greater than `keccak_rate` bytes, keccak rounds will be run manually to
            squeeze extra output data.
            Set function name as "KMAC" and enable full randomization of customization string (if
            applicable).
            '''
      stage: V2
      tests: ["{name}_long_msg_and_output"]
    }
    {
      name: burst_write
      desc: '''
            This is the same as the long_message test, except we burst-write chunks of the message
            into the msg_fifo, and disable intermediate status/CSR checks.
            '''
      stage: V2
      tests: ["{name}_burst_write"]
    }
    {
      name: test_vectors
      desc: '''
            These tests drive NIST test vectors for SHA3/SHAKE/KMAC into the design and check
            the output against the expected digest values.
            '''
      stage: V2
      tests: ["{name}_test_vectors_sha3_224", "{name}_test_vectors_sha3_256",
              "{name}_test_vectors_sha3_384", "{name}_test_vectors_sha3_512",
              "{name}_test_vectors_shake_128", "{name}_test_vectors_shake_256",
              "{name}_test_vectors_kmac", "{name}_test_vectors_kmac_xof"]
    }
    {
      name: sideload
      desc: '''
            Same as the smoke test, except we set cfg.sideload and provide a
            valid sideloaded key as well as a valid SW-provided key.
            KMAC should operate on the sideloaded key regardless of the cfg_shadowed.sideload field
            value.
            '''
      stage: V2
      tests: ["{name}_sideload"]
    }
    {
      name: app
      desc: '''
            Test that the Keymgr/ROM/LC can all initiate a KMAC operation through
            the application interface - Keymgr uses KMAC hash, while ROM/LC use CShake.
            Use an array of kmac_app_agents to send message data to the KMAC and to control
            the hashing logic, and set cfg.sideload if the Keymgr is enabled.
            The result digest sent to the kmac_app_agent will be compared against the result from
            the DPI reference model.
            In addition, read from the STATE window afterwards and confirm that this access is
            blocked and will return 0.
            '''
      stage: V2
      tests: ["{name}_app"]
    }
    {
      name: app_with_partial_data
      desc: '''
            Based on the kmac_app test, this test will send partial data from application interface
            by sending `strb` with values other than `8'hFF`.
            Because the scoreboard is cycle accurate and does not support this feature, this test
            will not check `status` and `intr_state` registers, but will check other registers and
            all interface data including digest.
            '''
      stage: V2
      tests: ["{name}_app_with_partial_data"]
   }
    {
      name: dynamic_app_sha3_smoke
      desc: '''
        Stimulus:
        Send SHA3-256 config and a short final message via the KMAC IP UVM app agent; program
        prerequisite CSRs through TL and send a separate zero-strobe termination.

        Checks:
            Count requests only at req_valid&&req_ready and responses only at rsp_valid&&rsp_ready.
            Check config-before-message, one message end, stable request fields while stalled, four
            ordered 64-bit digest beats against the message reference, error=0, then drain before
            accepting exactly one successful finish and no later digest/finish.
            '''
      stage: V2
      tests: []
    }
    {
      name: dynamic_app_sha3_strengths
      desc: '''
        Stimulus:
        Run independent dynamic SHA3-224/256/384/512 sessions with final message and separate
        zero-strobe termination.

        Checks:
        Check 4/4/6/8 accepted 64-bit digest beats in
            reference order, compare defined bits, require unused SHA3-224 transport bytes zero,
            and reject extra/duplicate beats. Drain digest before termination; require exactly one
            error-free finish and no post-finish response.
            '''
      stage: V2
      tests: []
    }
    {
      name: dynamic_app_fixed_output_xof
      desc: '''
        Stimulus:
        Run SHAKE and cSHAKE at L128/L256 with en_xof=0 and partial/full output up to the first
        rate.

        Checks:
        Check config/message handshake order, stable stalled requests, ordered
            reference output, and a maximum complete rate of 21/17 64-bit beats respectively, with
            no second squeeze. Drain output before separate termination and one successful finish;
            vary legal inter-request gaps.
            '''
      stage: V2
      tests: []
    }
    {
      name: dynamic_app_cshake_prefix
      desc: '''
        Stimulus:
        Program non-empty cSHAKE N/S via TL PREFIX writes before app config/message at L128/L256.

        Checks:
        Check each PREFIX write completes first, then config and one final-message
            handshake with stable stalled payload. Compare ordered accepted response beats to the
            reference using programmed N/S; drain before separate termination and one successful
            finish, with no trailing response.
            '''
      stage: V2
      tests: []
    }
    {
      name: dynamic_app_kmac
      desc: '''
        Stimulus:
        Run full dynamic KMAC with valid KeyMgr sideload key, legal strength, en_xof=0, and a
        known message. Before config establish full-KMAC build, entropy_ready, and key validity.

        Checks:
        Check ordered config/message/last handshakes and stall stability;
            compare eight 64-bit digest beats to the KMAC reference, error=0, and no duplicated
            key/prefix payload. Drain before separate zero-strobe termination and one successful
            finish; reject extra beats.
            '''
      stage: V2
      tests: []
    }
    {
      name: dynamic_app_kmac_partial_message
      desc: '''
        Stimulus:
        Run two fresh dynamic KMAC sessions with a valid KeyMgr sideload key, legal strength,
        en_xof=0, and the same known 11-byte message. Before config, establish full-KMAC build,
        entropy_ready, and key validity. In one session, end with a 3-byte partial beat
        (strb=0x07, req_last=1). In the other, send that partial beat with req_last=0, followed by
        an empty message-ending beat (strb=0, req_last=1).

        Checks:
        Check config-before-message order and count only accepted handshakes. Require the exact
            data/framing sequence in each session and stable payload during stalls. Compare all
            eight accepted 64-bit KMAC digest beats from both sessions against the reference for
            the same key, prefix, and 11 message bytes; require identical matching digests and
            error=0. After digest drain, send a distinct session-termination request
            (strb=0, req_last=0), then require exactly one successful finish and no extra beats.
            Do not confuse the empty message-ending beat with session termination.
            '''
      stage: V2
      tests: []
    }
    {
      name: dynamic_app_rate_boundaries
      desc: '''
        Stimulus:
        Test supported modes/strengths below, at, and above the Keccak rate with full and partial
        final beats, empty final messages, and separate post-digest zero-strobe termination. For
        partial messages, exercise both legal endings: a partial beat with req_last=1, and a
        partial beat with req_last=0 followed by an empty message-ending beat (strb=0, req_last=1).

        Checks:
        Count accepted handshakes; check byte order and LSB-contiguous partial strobes. Allow the
          partial non-final beat when it is followed by the empty message-ending beat; do not
          require every non-final beat to have a full strobe. Check both encodings produce the
          reference digest for the same message bytes. Distinguish the empty message-ending beat
          (strb=0, req_last=1) from the later session-termination request (strb=0, req_last=0),
          count each accepted request once, and check no extra end marker or response.
            '''
      stage: V2
      tests: []
    }
    {
      name: dynamic_app_empty_message
      desc: '''
        Stimulus:
        Send empty SHA3-256 (strb=0,last=1), accept its digest, then send a separate zero-strobe
        termination.

        Checks:
        Check config and exactly one empty-message request, distinct
            from termination; compare four accepted digest beats to the empty-string reference with
            error=0. Terminate only after digest drain and require one finish and no extra response.
            '''
      stage: V2
      tests: []
    }
    {
      name: dynamic_app_multibeat_message
      desc: '''
        Stimulus:
        Send a known 17-byte SHA3-256 message as two full beats and one 1-byte final beat, then
        terminate separately.

        Checks:
        Check config first; count only accepted transfers;
            verify full non-final strobes/last=0, final strb=0x01/last=1, stable stalled payload,
            order, and exactly 17 valid absorbed bytes. Compare four digest beats to reference and
            require one error-free finish after drain, with no duplicate response.
            '''
      stage: V2
      tests: []
    }
    {
      name: dynamic_app_partial_message
      desc: '''
        Stimulus:
        Send known 11-byte SHA3-256 input as one full beat and one 3-byte final beat.

        Checks:
        Check exactly two ordered message handshakes, final strb=0x07/last=1, stable payload
            under stalls, and only 11 valid bytes in the reference. Compare four digest beats and
            error=0; after digest drain handshake separate zero-strobe termination.
            '''
      stage: V2
      tests: []
    }
    {
      name: dynamic_app_cshake_prefix_programming
      desc: '''
        Stimulus:
        Program encoded cSHAKE N/S through TL PREFIX writes before dynamic app config/message.

        Checks:
        Check every TL write completes before config; dynamic config carries
            mode/strength but not N/S. Verify ordered stable config/message/final-message
            handshakes, reference output/count using exactly the programmed prefix, drain, separate
            termination, one successful finish, and no extra beats.
            '''
      stage: V2
      tests: []
    }
    {
      name: dynamic_app_response_backpressure
      desc: '''
        Stimulus:
        Stall first/last digest, error, and finish responses using deterministic and randomized
        ready policies, including configured max_rsp_ready_delay.

        Checks:
        For every response
            class hold payload (both digest shares, error, finish) stable from valid assertion until
            handshake; count handshakes only and detect loss, duplication, or ordering errors. Treat
            ready-delay as a testbench policy bound, not an IP latency guarantee; drain before finish.
            '''
      stage: V2
      tests: []
    }
    {
      name: dynamic_app_pipelined_termination
      desc: '''
        Stimulus:
        Terminate once with no response stalled and once with a valid digest stalled.

        Checks:
        Count request/response handshakes independently. In the stalled case allow at most one
            pending beat, stable until accepted exactly once and with no replacement. Drain pending
            data before exactly one finish; require no later response and do not assume immediate
            RTL response.
            '''
      stage: V2
      tests: []
    }
    {
      name: dynamic_app_xof_stream
      desc: '''
        Stimulus:
        Run SHAKE128 en_xof=1 through the first 21-beat rate and into the next squeeze.

        Checks:
        Compare the first 21 accepted beats in order to reference and ensure the next squeeze
            does not start before they handshake. Accept at least one correct next-rate beat; then
            terminate separately, drain, and require one error-free finish with no later output.
            '''
      stage: V2
      tests: []
    }
    {
      name: dynamic_app_xof_backpressure
      desc: '''
        Stimulus:
        Run SHAKE128 en_xof=1 with early and near-rate-boundary response stalls.

        Checks:
        Require valid and digest shares/error/finish flags stable until ready, count each beat
            once in reference order, and verify 21 first-rate beats before next squeeze. Resume
            within configured ready policy, prevent premature finish/extra beats, then terminate
            after next-rate data and drain to one finish.
            '''
      stage: V2
      tests: []
    }
    {
      name: dynamic_app_xof_pipelined_termination
      desc: '''
        Stimulus:
        Assert separate zero-strobe termination while a digest response is valid but stalled.

        Checks:
        Handshake termination once, hold pending response payload/flags stable until
            accepted exactly once, and prohibit replacement or duplicate beats. Drain offered data
            before one finish and check no post-finish response; apply the configured ready policy.
            '''
      stage: V2
      tests: []
    }
    {
      name: dynamic_app_sideloaded_kmac_output
      desc: '''
        Stimulus:
        Run dynamic KMAC with valid KeyMgr key, legal strength, en_xof=0. Optionally include the
        invalid en_xof=1 subcase.

        Checks:
        Check entropy_ready/key validity before config, unique ordered config/message/termination,
            eight ordered 64-bit digest beats against KMAC reference, no duplicated key/prefix in
            payload, error=0, and one finish after digest drain. For the invalid en_xof=1 subcase,
            expect error response, termination, successful finish, and no hash output or CSR error.
            '''
      stage: V2
      tests: []
    }
    {
      name: dynamic_app_entropy_prerequisite
      desc: '''
        Stimulus:
        For the masked KMAC case, start from reset and do not configure the entropy engine before
        requesting dynamic KMAC, so entropy_configured remains false. Do not rely on clearing the
        entropy_ready CSR after the entropy engine has already been configured. Also run a non-KMAC
        control. In an unmasked build, optionally clear entropy_ready through TL as a control; the
        internal entropy_configured signal is tied true in that build.

        Checks:
        In the masked case, verify KMAC config/message/error/termination/finish order, accepted-but-
          discarded message, error=1, no digest or CSR error/ack, then one error-free finish.
          Confirm the non-KMAC control returns its reference digest. In the unmasked build, expect
          the KMAC request to proceed normally and match its reference digest; do not expect an
          entropy-not-ready error because entropy_configured is tied true. Bound progress by
          watchdog, not fixed response latency.
            '''
      stage: V2
      tests: []
    }
    {
      name: dynamic_app_service_rejection
      desc: '''
        Stimulus:
        Send invalid mode-strength or illegal en_xof, complete required message phase, and
        terminate separately, isolated from key/fault errors.

        Checks:
        Check rejected message is not
            forwarded to hash engine; error follows accepted final-message request; then require
            one finish with error=0, no valid digest or new CSR error/IRQ/ack. Check stable stalled
            request fields and count only valid&&ready transfers.
            '''
      stage: V2
      tests: []
    }
    {
      name: dynamic_app_sideload_key_invalidation
      desc: '''
        Stimulus:
        In separate dynamic OTBN KMAC runs invalidate key-valid before and after PROCESS.

        Checks:
        Check ordered config/message/final handshakes, ErrKeyNotValid/kmac_err,
            documented error response and error-marked finish; discard invalid digest and do not
            duplicate PROCESS. TL-write 1 to clear kmac_err, issue CMD.err_processed as required,
            wait for recovery/ownership release, and verify a valid reference-checked follow-up;
            vary acknowledgement delay.
            '''
      stage: V2
      tests: []
    }
    {
      name: dynamic_app_recoverability
      desc: '''
        Stimulus:
        Exercise pure service rejection and invalid-key errors separately; vary key error phase
        and TL acknowledgement delay, then run a valid session.

        Checks:
        Check accepted
            handshake order. Service rejection requires error response, termination, successful
            finish, and no CSR ack. Key error requires ERR_CODE/kmac_err, phase-correct response and
            finish, TL clear then err_processed, observed recovery-busy then idle/owner release, and
            a reference-checked follow-up with no stale state or response.
            '''
      stage: V2
      tests: []
    }
    {
      name: dynamic_app_invalid_configuration
      desc: '''
        Stimulus:
        With legal CSR/key prerequisites, send illegal dynamic mode/strength or en_xof, complete
        required message phase, and terminate separately.

        Checks:
        Check one invalid
            config acceptance, no hash-engine forwarding, error response after final request,
            exactly one finish with error=0, no digest/extra response/CSR error/IRQ/ack, stable
            stalled requests, and watchdog-bounded progress rather than fixed latency.
            '''
      stage: V2
      tests: []
    }
    {
      name: dynamic_app_entropy_not_ready_error
      desc: '''
        Stimulus:
        In a masked build, start from reset and leave the entropy engine unconfigured before
        requesting otherwise-valid dynamic KMAC with a valid sideload key. Do not rely on clearing
        entropy_ready through TL after entropy has already been configured. Also run a non-KMAC
        control. In an unmasked build, optionally clear entropy_ready through TL as a control; its
        internal entropy_configured signal is tied true.

        Checks:
        For the masked case, verify KMAC rejection follows the accepted message with error=1,
          separate termination, one finish with error=0, and no digest/ERR_CODE/kmac_err/SW
          acknowledgement; the non-KMAC control returns its normal digest/finish. In an unmasked
          build, expect KMAC to complete normally with a reference-matching digest and finish,
          not an entropy-not-ready error. Use bounded progress checks, not exact response timing.
            '''
      stage: V2
      tests: []
    }
    {
      name: dynamic_app_invalid_key_error
      desc: '''
        Stimulus:
        In separate pre- and post-PROCESS dynamic OTBN KMAC runs, invalidate the key.

        Checks:
        Count valid&&ready transfers and verify ErrKeyNotValid/kmac_err and required error
        response/finish flags;
            do not compare digest or duplicate PROCESS. TL clear kmac_err then CMD.err_processed
            in order, wait for recovery-busy to idle/owner release, then verify valid follow-up
            digest/finish against reference.
            '''
      stage: V2
      tests: []
    }
    {
      name: dynamic_app_mixed_mode_stress
      desc: '''
        Stimulus:
        Run back-to-back SHA3/SHAKE/cSHAKE/KMAC sessions varying strengths, framing, request
        gaps, response stalls, and recoverable errors.

        Checks:
        Per session score accepted
            handshakes in order; check config-before-message, one message end, mode-specific digest
            beat count/content, error/finish semantics, and absence of lost/duplicate/stale traffic.
            Cover selected mode/strength/framing/error and owner release. Distinguish sequential
            stress from simultaneous arbitration; use configured agent bounds, not latency promises.
            '''
      stage: V2
      tests: []
    }
    {
      name: dynamic_app_multi_rate_xof
      desc: '''
        Stimulus:
        Run SHAKE/cSHAKE en_xof=1 through complete rates and at least one next-squeeze beat.

        Checks:
        For L128/L256 check each complete rate has 21/17 accepted beats in reference
            order and next squeeze starts only after the previous rate handshakes. Accept at least
            one next-rate beat, terminate once, drain, and require one error-free finish with no
            later output; use watchdog, not fixed permutation delay.
            '''
      stage: V2
      tests: []
    }
    {
      name: dynamic_app_concurrent_requesters
      desc: '''
        Stimulus:
        Drive OTBN dynamic/static app agents with TL SW START requests at directed same-cycle
        arbitration and command-collision offsets.

        Checks:
        Count accepted grants; verify
            documented priority, losing-request stall/stability, owner-only responses, and per-
            session digest/finish. Rejected SW commands report ErrSwIssuedCmdInAppActive and command
            info without ownership theft/state writes; clear error as needed and verify retry after
            release. Use watchdogs, not undocumented arbitration latency.
            '''
      stage: V2
      tests: []
    }
    {
      name: dynamic_app_reset_cancellation
      desc: '''
        Stimulus:
        Reset during config, message, processing, response stall, and finish in isolated runs.

        Checks:
        Record last accepted handshake; verify reset clears request/response
            valids and discards pending beats. Canceled sessions need no finish and must not emit
            stale responses. After reset run a fresh session and compare exact accepted-beat order/
            count and digest to reference; use reset synchronization and a testbench timeout.
            '''
      stage: V2
      tests: []
    }
    {
      name: dynamic_app_reset_during_response
      desc: '''
        Stimulus:
        Stall a digest/finish response (valid&&!ready), record payload/flags, then reset.

        Checks:
        Require stability only until reset and clearing after reset; no pre-reset beat
            may be accepted or replayed. Run fresh SHA3-256 and check config/message, four ordered
            digest beats, separate termination, exactly one successful finish, and bounded post-
            reset progress.
            '''
      stage: V2
      tests: []
    }
    {
      name: dynamic_app_fatal_escalation
      desc: '''
        Stimulus:
        Inject KMAC LC escalation independently during OTBN app config, message, processing,
        and response.

        Checks:
        Record accepted handshakes; require fatal alert/terminal
            lockout, no normal digest/finish or new app traffic after lockout, and specified fault
            behavior for pending outputs (not assumed graceful drain). CSR acknowledgement cannot
            recover; only IP reset permits a fresh session.
            '''
      stage: V2
      tests: []
    }
    {
      name: dynamic_app_fault_protection
      desc: '''
        Stimulus:
        Use scoped UVM fault injection on countermeasure targets and LC input during app
        config/message/process/response phases.

        Checks:
        For each fault/phase check specified
            assertion/alert, status/lockout, and response behavior; count only actual handshakes.
            Do not require defined digest, normal finish, or graceful response for terminal faults.
            Check no unintended post-fault traffic and reset recovery only where specified; keep
            fatal cases separate from recoverable service/key errors and avoid unsupported latency
            assumptions.
            '''
      stage: V2
      tests: []
    }
    {
      name: context_restore_non_idle
      desc: '''
        Stimulus:
        With sideload disabled, attempt STATE_WRITE during SW absorb/squeeze and then write
        STATE.

        Checks:
        Confirm STATUS phase before command, each TL response, ErrSwCmdSequence/
            kmac_err, state_write=0, and no effective state writes. Finish and reference-check the
            original hash; clear kmac_err, issue legal DONE, and run a fresh hash.
            '''
      stage: V2
      tests: []
    }
    {
      name: context_stop_after_squeeze
      desc: '''
        Stimulus:
        With sideload disabled, poll STATUS.sha3_squeeze through completed TL reads, then issue
        CMD.STOP.

        Checks:
        Verify TL completion, ErrSwCmdSequence/kmac_err, sha3_stopped=0,
            and preserved digest. Clear kmac_err and issue DONE in legal order; verify a fresh hash
            completes. Do not expect CMD.err_processed.
            '''
      stage: V2
      tests: []
    }
    {
      name: context_continue_without_claim
      desc: '''
        Stimulus:
        With sideload disabled and KMAC idle, issue CONTINUE without first issuing STATE_WRITE.

        Checks:
        Require ErrSwCmdSequence in ERR_CODE and kmac_err. Confirm CONTINUE is rejected: the
            engine remains idle, no absorb/process transition occurs, and the Keccak state is
            unchanged. Clear kmac_err, then run a fresh legal SW hash through DONE and check its
            digest against the reference to prove recovery.
            '''
      stage: V2
      tests: []
    }
    {
      name: context_commands_while_stopped
      desc: '''
        Stimulus:
        With sideload disabled, run a separate trial for each command. In each trial, start an SW
        hash, wait until a rate-aligned message block is absorbed, issue STOP, and wait for
        STATUS.sha3_stopped. Then issue one CMD command other than DONE: START, PROCESS, RUN, STOP,
        STATE_WRITE, or CONTINUE.

        Checks:
        For every command, require ErrSwCmdSequence in ERR_CODE and kmac_err. Confirm the command
            is rejected and the engine remains stopped: STATUS.sha3_stopped stays set, the saved
            Keccak state is unchanged, and no new absorb, resume, or state-write transition occurs.
            Clear kmac_err and issue the only legal cleanup command, DONE; verify return to idle.
            After each trial, run a fresh legal SW hash to completion and check its digest against
            the reference to prove recovery. Do not require CMD.err_processed for these
            command-sequence errors.
            '''
      stage: V2
      tests: []
    }
    {
      name: context_restore_cfg
      desc: '''
        Stimulus:
        Save a SW context through STOP/STOPPED/STATE reads/DONE, run another config, then
        restore original CFG, claim STATE_WRITE, load shares, and CONTINUE.

        Checks:
        STOP only after rate-aligned absorption; poll STOPPED before reading shares and complete
            reads before DONE. Restore CFG before claim. On STATE_WRITE, check the clear control
            and directly observe that internal Keccak storage (both shares when masked) is zero
            before the first state-word write; do not use a zero STATE-window read as evidence.
            Then load all shares before CONTINUE and compare the resumed digest to reference.
            '''
      stage: V2
      tests: []
    }
    {
      name: context_abandoned_partial_restore
      desc: '''
            Stimulus:
            In a masked and an unmasked build, restore CFG and issue STATE_WRITE. Before loading
            state, observe the internal Keccak state directly. Write only selected nonzero state
            words, leaving the restore incomplete, then issue DONE without CONTINUE.

            Checks:
            Verify STATE_WRITE asserts the clear control and internal Keccak storage is zero
            before any state writes. Confirm the selected writes make the state nonzero, then check
            DONE clears the internal state storage to zero (both shares when masked) and releases
            STATE_WRITE access. Do not treat a zero read from an inaccessible STATE window as proof
            of clearing; check the underlying state storage directly.
            '''
      stage: V2
      tests: []
    }
    {
      name: context_repeated_checkpoints
      desc: '''
            Stimulus:
            Repeatedly absorb full user-message rate blocks, STOP, wait STOPPED, save both shares in
            TB buffers, and DONE. At checkpoints choose no intervening operation, a full independent
            SW hash, or a full app session; restore CFG, claim STATE_WRITE, reload the latest
            checkpoint, and CONTINUE before final PROCESS.

            Checks:
            Enforce exact order and 50 words per share, no missing/duplicate TL traffic, correct
            ownership/status, and reference-matching final digest. Cover each switch choice and
            multiple checkpoints.
            '''
      stage: V2
      tests: []
    }
    {
      name: state_app_stalled_through_continue
      desc: '''
            Stimulus:
            Save an aligned SW context by issuing STOP, waiting for STOPPED, reading both state
            shares, and issuing DONE. Restore the saved CFG, issue STATE_WRITE, and wait for the
            claim. While loading the state shares, have a hardware application interface request
            service. Complete state loading, issue CONTINUE, and resume the SW hash through its
            remaining message and PROCESS; issue DONE only after the SW operation is complete.

            Checks:
            Verify the app request remains stalled and ungranted during state loading, after
            CONTINUE closes STATE_WRITE access, and throughout resumed SW hashing. Confirm
            CONTINUE does not transfer block ownership: the app remains stalled until SW issues
            DONE. Then require the pending app request to be granted and complete exactly once, with
            correct response routing and reference-checked SW/app digests; no request may be lost,
            duplicated, or accepted before DONE.
            '''
      stage: V2
      tests: []
    }
    {
      name: context_entropy_prerequisite
      desc: '''
            Stimulus:
            In masked full-KMAC RTL with sideload=0, save an aligned checkpoint, reset, restore CFG
            with entropy_ready=0, claim STATE_WRITE, load shares, and issue CONTINUE.

            Checks:
            Verify TL order, ErrSwHashingWithoutEntropyReady, kmac_err/IRQ, CONTINUE blocked, no
            absorb, and claim held. Clear kmac_err then DONE to release; configure valid entropy,
            repeat restore/CONTINUE, finalize, and compare whole-message digest. No err_processed.
            '''
      stage: V2
      tests: []
    }
    {
      name: context_sideload_restriction
      desc: '''
            Stimulus:
            With sideload=1, isolate STOP during SW absorption, STATE_WRITE while idle, and CONTINUE.

            Checks:
            Check TL completion, ErrSwSaveRestoreSideload (including rejected command), kmac_err/IRQ
            if enabled, and no valid checkpoint/claim/resume/state write. Clear kmac_err before legal
            cleanup/follow-up and check original hash/status as appropriate.
            '''
      stage: V2
      tests: []
    }
    {
      name: context_kmac_key_prefix_continuity
      desc: '''
            Stimulus:
            With sideload disabled, program key/prefix through TL, absorb a full user-message rate
            block, STOP/save/DONE, then claim/load/CONTINUE without replaying key/prefix.

            Checks:
            Complete key/prefix writes before START; count only user bytes for alignment; wait
            STOPPED before reads and state_write before reload. Append output-length encoding once
            before PROCESS and compare to one-pass KMAC reference; require no duplicate commands/
            writes or errors.
            '''
      stage: V2
      tests: []
    }
    {
      name: context_reset_interruption
      desc: '''
            Stimulus:
            Reset during STOP/STOPPED, STATE reads, DONE, claim, partial state loading within/
            between shares, and before/after CONTINUE.

            Checks:
            Track completed TL and command handshakes; verify reset defaults, claim/write-enable
            cleared, pending work canceled, and no spontaneous continuation or stale response. TB
            save buffers persist. After reset run fresh START/message/PROCESS/read/DONE and compare
            digest to reference.
            '''
      stage: V2
      tests: []
    }
    {
      name: context_save_alignment
      desc: '''
            Stimulus:
            Track SW user-message bytes since START/CONTINUE; issue TL STOP at aligned and
            misaligned lengths with varied FIFO/absorption timing. Include a directed aligned case
            where software has delivered a complete rate block through TL but internal absorption
            is still outstanding when STOP arrives. Before each STOP trial, clear
            INTR_STATE.kmac_done; run completion with kmac_done enabled and disabled separately.

            Checks:
            Count only user-message bytes accepted/absorbed; exclude key/prefix and distinguish TL
            FIFO acceptance from SHA3 absorption. In the outstanding-absorption case, verify STOP
            does not lose or duplicate pending data: the complete rate block is absorbed before the
            context is saved, and the resumed operation produces the uninterrupted reference
            digest. Check STATUS.sha3_stopped and the kmac_done event from STOP: INTR_STATE.kmac_done
            must latch in both enable settings, while the external interrupt is asserted only when
            INTR_ENABLE.kmac_done is set. For other aligned inputs, save both shares and verify
            resume. For misaligned input, check the TL response, ErrSwStopNotBlockAligned/kmac_err,
            invalid-save behavior, then clear the error and issue DONE before follow-up.
            '''
      stage: V2
      tests: []
    }
    {
      name: state_app_ownership_reads
      desc: '''
            Stimulus:
            Read both STATE apertures through TL while each app owns KMAC during message,
            processing, and response phases.

            Checks:
            Count each TL request/response; verify app-owned read data matches specified zero
            behavior with no state leak. Ensure reads do not stall/corrupt app protocol; preserve
            response order/count and reference-matching digest.
            '''
      stage: V2
      tests: []
    }
    {
      name: state_share0_address_range
      desc: '''
            Stimulus:
            In masked mode claim STATE_WRITE and write aligned share0 first/last words; test both
            state-endianness settings and distinct first/middle/last patterns. Optionally sweep all
            valid words.

            Checks:
            Confirm claim before writes and each TL completion; internal write index/share0 enable/
            endian conversion must be correct with no neighbor changes, and share1 must remain
            unchanged. A full sweep covers all 50 words in 0x400..0x4C4. Do not read back while
            claimed; release with DONE.
            '''
      stage: V2
      tests: []
    }
    {
      name: state_share1_address_range
      desc: '''
            Stimulus:
            In a masked build claim STATE_WRITE and write distinct aligned share1 first, middle,
            and last words; optionally sweep all valid words.

            Checks:
            Confirm STATUS.state_write before writes, TL completion, and internal accepted addresses/
            share select in 0x500..0x5C4. Share0 remains unchanged; check no alias. A full sweep
            covers 50 words. Avoid claim-time readback; optionally CONTINUE/finalize valid state and
            compare reference.
            '''
      stage: V2
      tests: []
    }
    {
      name: state_valid_invalid_boundaries
      desc: '''
            Stimulus:
            Read/write aligned valid and invalid selectors in both STATE apertures, including the
            inter-share gap 0x4C8..0x4FC and share1 tail 0x5C8..0x5FC. Exercise reads when visible
            and writes with/without claim.

            Checks:
            Count one TL request/response per address; cover 50 valid words per 64-slot aperture.
            Invalid selectors read zero and writes must not write, alias, or wrap. Compare internal
            accepted writes with intended valid writes; avoid claim-time readback and misaligned
            0x4C7.
            '''
      stage: V2
      tests: []
    }
    {
      name: state_app_ownership_arbitration
      desc: '''
            Stimulus:
            Drive app requests against TL STATE_WRITE and CMD.STOP during app ownership and at the
            same-cycle app-grant boundary; retry STATE_WRITE after app completion.

            Checks:
            Count TL responses and app grants. Rejected SW commands report
            ErrSwIssuedCmdInAppActive/command info, keep state_write low, cause no effective STATE
            writes, and preserve app response routing/digest. Clear kmac_err as required, wait for
            owner release, then verify STATE_WRITE retry succeeds.
            '''
      stage: V2
      tests: []
    }
    {
      name: state_partial_write_with_claim
      desc: '''
            Stimulus:
            In a masked build claim STATE_WRITE and issue legal TL partial-mask writes to valid
            STATE words.

            Checks:
            After confirmed claim, require one ordered TL D-channel error per request and zero
            accepted/effective internal writes or state mutation. Distinguish TL adapter rejection
            from KMAC ERR_CODE/kmac_err/IRQ; do not expect a KMAC error.
            '''
      stage: V2
      tests: []
    }
    {
      name: state_partial_write_without_claim
      desc: '''
            Stimulus:
            Without STATE_WRITE claim, issue legal partial-mask writes to valid STATE words under
            varied ownership.

            Checks:
            Keep STATUS.state_write=0; require one adapter D-channel error per completed TL write
            and zero internal state writes. Do not infer KMAC ERR_CODE/kmac_err/IRQ from TL adapter
            errors.
            '''
      stage: V2
      tests: []
    }
    {
      name: state_write_without_claim
      desc: '''
            Stimulus:
            Without STATE_WRITE ownership, issue full-word writes to both valid shares during idle
            and active operations.

            Checks:
            Verify state_write=0 before each transaction and distinguish TL response from internal
            write-enable. Require no internal write or write-induced state/digest while idle; allow
            only legitimate engine evolution while hashing. Check both shares and follow-up digest
            against reference.
            '''
      stage: V2
      tests: []
    }
    {
      name: state_claim_while_sha3_busy
      desc: '''
            Stimulus:
            During TL-driven SW absorption with sideload disabled, issue STATE_WRITE and subsequent
            full-word STATE writes.

            Checks:
            Confirm STATUS.sha3_absorb before STATE_WRITE; check TL completion, ErrSwCmdSequence/
            kmac_err, state_write=0, and no accepted internal writes. Preserve original reference
            digest; clear kmac_err, PROCESS/read digest/DONE in legal order, and run a clean follow-
            up hash.
            '''
      stage: V2
      tests: []
    }
   {
      name: entropy_refresh
      desc: ''' Test entropy interface for KMAC.

            This test randomly chooses to execute either a SW-controlled hash or a hash from the
            App interface.
            All configuration fields are left as is, but now we will request EDN entropy by setting
            the following registers with random value:
            - "cmd.entropy_req": Request an EDN entropy
            - "cmd.hash_cnt_clear": Clear the entropy_refresh_hash_cnt
            - "entropy_refresh_threshold_shadowed": The threshold when KMAC should request an EDN
              entropy

            In masked mode, scoreboard will check:
            - Register `entropy_refresh_hash_cnt` value
            - KMAC requests EDN at the correct time

            In unmasked mode, scoreboard will check:
            - Register `entropy_refresh_hash_cnt` always returns 0
            '''
      stage: V2
      tests: ["{name}_entropy_refresh"]
   }
   {
      name: error
      desc: '''
            Try several error sequences:

            - Update key/prefix/config during absorption/process/squeeze stage.
            - Write msg to msg_fifo during process/squeeze stage
            - When in KMAC mode, set the function name to not "KMAC"
            - Incorrect SHA3 control flow:
              - Issue Process/Run/Done cmds before issuing Start
              - Issue Run/Done before issuing Process
              - Issue Start after issuing Process
              - If squeezing data, issue Start/Process after issuing Run
            - Incorrect KMAC configurations (e.g. set KMAC strength as 512).
            - Provide software inputs during operation of the application interface
            '''
      stage: V2
      tests: ["{name}_error"]
    }
    {
      name: key_error
      desc: ''' Test kmac responses correctly when keymgr app sends request but key is not valid.
            **Stimulus**:
            - Configure kmac and send keymgr request, but did not set valid bit for keymgr key
              input.
            - Wait randomly clock cycles.
            - Issue `err_processed`, then wait for the keymgr interface handshake to finish.
            - Repeat the above sequence a few times.
            - Issue correct kmac request from app or sw interface.

            **Check**:
            - Check error related registers including `err_code`, `status`, and `interrupt`.
            - Check keymgr interface responses with all zero digests and error bit is set.
            - Check kmac can resume normal functionalities after processing this error case.
            '''
      stage: V2
      tests: ["{name}_key_error"]
    }
    {
      name: sideload_invalid
      desc: ''' Test kmac responses correctly sideloaded key is invalidated.
            **Stimulus**:
            - Configure kmac and send keymgr request.
            - Wait until random FSM app state is entered.
            - Invalidate the sideloaded key by forcing the .valid signal to 0.
            - Repeat the above sequence a few times to try different configurations.

            **Check**:
            - Check error related registers including `err_code`.
            - Check keymgr interface have responses where all digests are zero and the error
              bit is set.
            - Check kmac can resume normal functionalities after processing this error case.
            '''
      stage: V2
      tests: ["{name}_sideload_invalid"]
    }
    {
      name: edn_timeout_error
      desc: ''' Test kmac responses correctly when EDN response timeout.
            Based on kmac app sequence, this sequence configures kmac to ensure an EDN timeout
            error by writing `entropy_period.wait_timer` and `entropy_period.prescaler` registers.
            **Check**:
            - Check error related registers including `err_code`, `status`, and `interrupt`.
            - Check keymgr interface responses with error bit sets to 1.
            - Check kmac can resume normal functionalities after processing this error case.
            - Check timeout error will not trigger if the `entropy_mode` is set to SW, or masking
              is disabled.
            '''
      stage: V2
      tests: ["{name}_edn_timeout_error"]
    }
    {
      name: entropy_mode_error
      desc: ''' Test kmac responses correctly when entropy mode is configured incorrectly.
            Based on kmac edn_timeout sequence, this sequence write incorrect
            `ral.cfg_shadowed.entropy_mode` before entropy is fetched.
            **Check**:
            - Check error related registers including `err_code`, `status`, and `interrupt`.
            - Check keymgr interface responses with error bit sets to 1.
            - Check kmac can resume normal functionalities after processing this error case.
            - Check timeout error will not trigger if masking is disabled.
            '''
      stage: V2
      tests: ["{name}_entropy_mode_error"]
   }
    {
      name: entropy_ready_error
      desc: ''' Test kmac responses correctly when entropy_ready field is not set.
            Based on kmac app sequence, this sequence does not write `1` to
            `ral.cfg_shadowed.entropy_ready` field before a kmac operation.
            **Check**:
            - If masking is enabled and the kmac operation is EDN mode:
              - If it is a SW operation, check error related registers including `err_code`,
                `status`, and `interrupt`.
              - If it is an APP operation, check keymgr interface responses with error bit sets to
                `1`.
            - If masking is not enabled or the operation does not require entropy:
              - Check no error triggered.
            '''
      stage: V2
      tests: ["{name}_entropy_ready_error"]
   }
   {
      name: lc_escalation
      desc: '''
            Randomly set `lc_escalate_en` to value that is not `Off` during Kmac operations.

            **Checks**:
            - Fatal alert fires continuously until reset is issued.
            - Status fields: `alert_fatal_fault` is set to 1 and `sha3_idle` is reset to 0.
            - Kmac does not accept any SW or APP requests.
            - Digest window always output all 0s.
            '''
      stage: V2
      tests: ["{name}_lc_escalation"]
    }
    {
      name: stress_all
      desc: '''
            - Combine above sequences in one test to run sequentially, except csr sequence and
              some error tests that disabled scoreboard.
            - Randomly add reset between each sequence'''
      stage: V2
      tests: ["kmac_stress_all"]
    }
    {
      name: throughput
      desc: '''
            Measure the throughput of the various hashing calculations and make sure they correspond
            to the expected throughput range for the design.
            '''
      stage: V3
      tests: ["{name}_throughput"]
    }
  ]

  // Coverage target: intended stimulus, event, or expected outcome to exercise. Failure
  // observation: an unexpected/missing response, mismatch, stale state, or other checker failure.
  // Points with both kinds of bins carry both labels.
  covergroups: [
    {
      name: config_cg
      desc: '''
            Covers that all valid configuration settings for the KMAC have been tested.
            Individual config settings [coverage target] that will be covered include:
            - hashing mode (sha3/shake/kmac) [coverage target]
            - security strength (128/224/256/384/512) [coverage target]
            - key length (128/192/256/384/512) [coverage target]
            - message endianness enable/disable [coverage target]
            - digest endianness enable/disable [coverage target]
            - XOF mode when using KMAC hashing [coverage target]
            All valid combinations of the above will also be crossed.
            '''
    }
    {
      name: msg_len_cg
      desc: '''
            Covers various input message length ranges [coverage target], to ensure that KMAC can operate
            successfully
            on different sized messages.
            The minimum tested msg length is 0 bytes, and the maximum length is 10_000 bytes,
            we will cover that an acceptable distribution of lengths has been seen [coverage target], and specifically
            cover some corner cases (like length 0).
            '''
    }
    {
      name: output_digest_len_cg
      desc: '''
            Similar to the `msg_len_cg`, we also want to cover various output digest lengths [coverage target] to
            ensure that KMAC can successfully produce outputs of varying sizes.
            Note that this only applies to XOF functions, as SHA3 functions have a fixed output
            length.
            '''
    }
    {
      name: prefix_range_cg
      desc: '''
            The prefix used for CSHAKE and KMAC (function_name + customization_string) [coverage target] are only
            allowed to be valid alphabet letters, or a space character.
            This covergroup covers that all of these characters have appeared in a prefix value.
            '''
    }
    {
      name: msgfifo_write_mask_cg
      desc: '''
            Covers that the msgfifo has been written using all possible TLUL masks [coverage target].
            '''
    }
    {
      name: msgfifo_level_cg
      desc: '''
            Covers that all possible FIFO statuses have been seen [coverage target] when running different hash
            operations (like sha3/shake/cshake/kmac), such as various fifo depths, fifo full, and
            fifo empty.
            '''
    }
    {
      name: sha3_status_cg
      desc: '''
            Covers that all SHA3-related status fields have eventually been seen [coverage target].
            '''
    }
    {
      name: state_read_mask_cg
      desc: '''
            Covers that the state windows have been read using all possible TLUL masks [coverage target].
            '''
    }
    {
      name: cmd_process_cg
      desc: '''
            Covers `CmdProcess` commands [coverage target] during the following sets
            of scenarios:
            - various msgfifo status (full/empty/in between) [coverage target]
            - while Keccak rounds are currently active/inactive [coverage target]
            '''
    }
    {
      name: sideload_cg
      desc: '''
            Covers scenarios where the sideloaded key is provided [coverage target] and should be
            used (`en_sideload==1`, `app_mode==AppKeymgr`), and scenarios where the sideloaded key
            is provided but should not be used.
            '''
    }
    {
      name: app_cg
      desc: '''
            Covers several scenarios related to the app interface:
            - A single data beat is sent [coverage target]
            - All partial data lengths have been seen [coverage target] (only applies to the last data beat)
            - Expected errors are reported through this interface [coverage target]
            - `Done` signal is sent [coverage target] while Keccak rounds are currently active/inactive [coverage target]

            Note that this covergroup will be duplicated once per app interface.
            '''
    }
    {
      name: app_sw_cfg_cg
      desc: '''
            Covers several scenarios related to the app interface and cfg_shadowed register value:
            - Hash_mode field value [coverage target].
            - Kmac_en field value [coverage target].
            - Kstrength field value [coverage target].

            These sw register settings should not affect kmac app interface calculation.
            Note that this covergroup will be duplicated once per app interface.
            '''
   }
   {
      name: entropy_timer_cg
      desc: '''
            Cover the values for the entropy_period register's `prescaler` and `wait_timer` fields
            [coverage target]. Cross these field ranges with whether the entropy EDN mode is on or off
            [coverage target].
            '''
   }
   {
      name: error_cg
      desc: '''
            Covers all expected error outcomes [coverage target]:
            - ErrKeyNotValid: secret key is invalid when KeyMgr initiates App operation [coverage target]
            - ErrSwPushedMsgFifo: SW writes the msgfifo while App interface is active [coverage target]
            - ErrSwIssuedCmdInAppActive: SW writes all possible commands to the KMAC
                                         while App interface is active
                                         [coverage target]
            - ErrWaitTimerExpired: KMAC timed out while waiting for EDN entropy [coverage target]
            - ErrIncorrectEntropyMode: incorrect entropy mode is detected by KMAC [coverage target]
            - ErrUnexpectedModeStrength: illegal mode/strength is selected [coverage target], and all but
                                         128/256 bit strengths are seen for XOF functions
                                         [coverage target]
            - ErrIncorrectFunctionName: function name is configured incorrectly [coverage target]
                                        when KMAC mode is enabled
                                         [coverage target]
            - ErrSwCmdSequence: SW issues commands to KMAC out of order [coverage target]; expected
                               error is checked [coverage target]
            '''
    }
    {
      name: dynamic_app_config_cg
      desc: '''
            Dynamic-app configuration and mode coverage. Keep mode/strength/en_xof bins and
        configuration acceptance together with their configuration crosses. Each listed point
        name is a stable identifier used by the groups crosses; the following values specify
        its required bins.

            Coverage requirements (classification in brackets):
            - dynamic_app_id [coverage target]: OTBN dynamic interface; static app IDs as controls where
              useful.
            - dynamic_mode [coverage target]: SHA3; SHAKE; cSHAKE; KMAC.
            - dynamic_strength [coverage target]: L128; L224; L256; L384; L512 as legal for the selected mode.
            - en_xof [coverage target]: 0; 1.
            - config_request_seen [coverage target]: config accepted; config rejected.
            - config_error [coverage target; failure observation]: no config error; expected config error;
              unexpected/missing config error.
            - finish [coverage target]: successful finish observed; finish absent due to cancellation/fault.
            - previous_mode [coverage target]: mode used by prior session.
            - current_mode [coverage target]: mode used by current session.

            Crosses:
            - dynamic_app_id_x_mode; mode_x_strength; mode_x_strength_x_en_xof;
              mode_x_en_xof_x_finish; mode_x_strength_x_en_xof_x_config_error;
              previous_mode_x_current_mode.
            '''
    }
    {
      name: dynamic_app_message_framing_cg
      desc: '''
            Dynamic-app message framing coverage. Sample message length/framing and final-beat
            strobes together, including the empty-message case.

            Coverage requirements (classification in brackets):
            - dynamic_mode [coverage target]: SHA3; SHAKE; cSHAKE; KMAC.
            - beat_count [coverage target]: 1; 2; 3+ beats.
            - message_beat_count [coverage target]: 1; 2; 3+ beats.
            - final_beat_strobe_bytes [coverage target]: 0; 1-7 partial bytes; full beat.
            - empty_message [coverage target]: empty final message; non-empty message.

            Crosses:
            - beat_count_x_final_strobe; empty_message_x_mode_x_finish.
            '''
    }
    {
      name: dynamic_app_prefix_cg
      desc: '''
            Dynamic-app prefix coverage. Sample function-name and customization-string lengths
            for empty, non-empty, boundary, and maximum cases.

            Coverage requirements (classification in brackets):
            - dynamic_mode [coverage target]: SHA3; SHAKE; cSHAKE; KMAC.
            - function_name_length [coverage target]: empty; non-empty; boundary/max.
            - customization_length [coverage target]: empty; non-empty; boundary/max.

            Cross:
            - mode_x_function_name_length_x_customization_length.
            '''
    }
    {
      name: dynamic_app_key_entropy_cg
      desc: '''
            Dynamic-app key and entropy prerequisite coverage, separate from ordinary configuration
            and response flow.

            Coverage requirements (classification in brackets):
            - dynamic_mode [coverage target]: SHA3; SHAKE; cSHAKE; KMAC.
            - key_source [coverage target]: software key; sideload key.
            - key_source_and_validity [coverage target]: software key; sideload valid; sideload
              invalidated.
            - key_validity [coverage target]: valid; invalidated; not applicable.
            - entropy_ready [coverage target]: 0; 1.
            - entropy_mode [coverage target]: software; EDN; idle/invalid where applicable.
            - masking_mode [coverage target]: masking enabled; masking disabled.
            - app_error [coverage target; failure observation]: no app error; expected app error;
              unexpected/missing app error.

            Crosses:
            - mode_x_key_source_x_key_validity_x_app_error;
              masking_x_entropy_mode_x_entropy_ready_x_app_error.
            '''
    }
    {
      name: dynamic_app_response_stream_cg
      desc: '''
            Dynamic-app response streaming and backpressure coverage. Track accepted output chunks,
            termination with pending responses, finish behavior, and configured ready policy.

            Coverage requirements (classification in brackets):
            - dynamic_mode [coverage target]: SHA3; SHAKE; cSHAKE; KMAC.
            - dynamic_strength [coverage target]: L128; L224; L256; L384; L512 as legal for selected mode.
            - response_chunk_count [coverage target]: single chunk; multiple chunks; full rate; additional squeeze.
            - pending_response_count [coverage target]: 0 pending; 1 pending; 2+ pending.
            - response_finish_observed [coverage target]: finish observed; finish absent due to reset/fatal event.
            - finish_error [coverage target]: finish error=0; finish error=1.
            - policy [coverage target]: deterministic; randomized; configured maximum delay.
            - response_stall_length [coverage target]: 0; 1; 2-to-bound; at maximum configured bound.
            - configured_stall_bound [coverage target]: configured max_rsp_ready_delay value.
            - observed_stall [coverage target; failure observation]: no stall; below bound; at bound; exceeds bound.
            - acceptance_point [coverage target]: response accepted within configured policy; acceptance delayed.
            - chunk_ordinal [coverage target]: first; middle; last; next-rate chunk.

            Crosses:
            - mode_x_strength_x_chunk_count; pending_response_count_x_finish_error;
              policy_x_stall_length_x_chunk_ordinal;
              configured_bound_x_observed_stall_x_acceptance_point.
            '''
    }
    {
      name: dynamic_app_concurrent_arbitration_cg
      desc: '''
            Dynamic app concurrent-arbitration coverage, separate from protocol and data-path bins.
            Cover concurrent requester combinations, documented arbitration outcomes, collision
            timing, and response routing.

            Coverage requirements (classification in brackets):
            - concurrent_request_set [coverage target]: dynamic OTBN only; static app only; SW only;
              dynamic OTBN+
              static app; dynamic OTBN+SW; static app+SW; all three concurrent.
            - request_set [coverage target]: same requester-set bins used by the arbitration cross.
            - arbitration_result [coverage target]: each documented priority winner; lower-priority request
              rejected
              or deferred; no eligible winner.
            - collision_timing [coverage target]: same-cycle; adjacent-cycle; non-overlapping.
            - response_route [coverage target]: dynamic OTBN interface; static app interface; SW interface; no
              response expected.

            Cross:
            - request_set_x_collision_timing_x_arbitration_result.
            '''
    }
    {
      name: dynamic_app_sustained_stress_cg
      desc: '''
            Dynamic app sustained-stress coverage, separate from per-session protocol bins. Track run
            length, gaps, overall transaction outcome, and mode transitions across sessions.

            Coverage requirements (classification in brackets):
            - test_session_run_length [coverage target]: 1 session; 2-10 sessions; 11-100 sessions;
              more than 100.
            - run_length [coverage target]: 1; 2-10; 11-100; more than 100 sessions.
            - inter_session_gap [coverage target]: back-to-back; 1 cycle; 2 or more cycles.
            - mode_transition [coverage target]: same mode; mode changed; transition to/from KMAC.
            - stress_run_result [coverage target; failure observation]: all requests completed; lost transaction;
              duplicate transaction;
              digest mismatch; timeout/deadlock; residual state detected.

            Cross:
            - run_length_x_mode_transition_x_inter_session_gap.
            '''
    }
    {
      name: dynamic_app_response_boundaries_cg
      desc: '''
            Dynamic app response-boundary coverage, separate from stream-level output accounting. Sample
            first/middle/last digest beats, error and finish responses against stall and boundary
            events.

            Coverage requirements (classification in brackets):
            - boundary_response_kind [coverage target]: first digest beat; middle digest beat; last digest
              beat; error
              response; finish response.
            - boundary_stall_event [coverage target]: no stall; termination during stall; key invalidation during
              stall; reset during stall.
            - response_stall_length [coverage target]: 0; 1; 2-to-bound; at maximum configured bound.
            - response_kind [coverage target]: first/middle/last digest; error; finish.
            - stall_event [coverage target]: no stall; termination; key invalidation; reset during stall.
            - stall_length [coverage target]: 0; 1; 2-to-bound; at maximum configured bound.

            Cross:
            - response_kind_x_stall_length_x_stall_event.
            '''
    }
    {
      name: dynamic_app_recovery_cg
      desc: '''
            Dynamic app recovery coverage, separate from mode, framing, and response-stream bins.
            Cover recoverable
            error classes and follow-up outcomes, repeated failures, software acknowledgement
            delay, and fatal lockout/recovery.

            Coverage requirements (classification in brackets):
            - recoverable_error_class [coverage target]: configuration error; invalid key; entropy not ready;
              application error; recoverable bus/protocol error.
            - post_error_operation_result [coverage target; failure observation]: valid follow-up accepted;
              follow-up digest matches
              reference; follow-up rejected; digest mismatch; timeout.
            - consecutive_failure_count [coverage target]: 1 failure; 2 consecutive failures; 3 or more consecutive.
            - software_ack_delay [coverage target]: no delay; short delay; maximum supported delay; acknowledgement
              timeout.
            - fatal_lockout_state [coverage target]: not escalated; escalated and blocked until reset; reset clears
              lockout; operation succeeds after reset.
            - fatal_escalation [coverage target]: absent; asserted; remains locked out until reset.
            - dynamic_app_id [coverage target]: OTBN dynamic interface; static app IDs as controls where useful.
            - key_source_and_validity [coverage target]: software key; sideload valid; sideload invalidated.
            - key_validity [coverage target]: valid; invalidated; not applicable.
            - error_response [coverage target; failure observation]: no error response; expected error
              response; missing/unexpected error.
            - injection_state [coverage target]: no injection; injection during active session.
            - dynamic_session [coverage target]: no session; config; message; processing; response.
            - fault_or_escalation_session_state [coverage target]: no session; config; message;
              processing; response.
            - terminal_outcome [coverage target]: recoverable completion; fatal lockout;
              reset recovery.
            - recovery_result [coverage target; failure observation]: recovered; recovery failed; timed out.
            - request_before_reset [coverage target]: accepted; blocked during fatal lockout.
            - request_after_reset [coverage target; failure observation]: accepted after reset; still blocked after reset.

            Crosses:
            - error_class_x_failure_count_x_followup_result;
              fatal_escalation_x_request_before_reset_x_request_after_reset;
              app_id_x_key_validity_x_error_response_x_recovery;
              injection_state_x_dynamic_session_x_terminal_outcome.
            - Keep requests observed during fatal lockout and blocked requests after completed reset
              visible so the lockout/recovery checker can fail them.
            '''
    }
    {
      name: dynamic_app_reset_coverage_cg
      desc: '''
            Dynamic app reset coverage, separate from regular session and response-stream bins.
            Track the response
            state when reset occurs, reset phase, pending response kind, stale-response behavior,
            and the result of the next session.

            Coverage requirements (classification in brackets):
            - reset_response_phase [coverage target]: no response pending; valid stalled; pending response; after
              accepted response.
            - pending_response_kind [coverage target; failure observation]: none; digest; error; finish; phase-inconsistent response.
            - dynamic_reset_phase [coverage target]: configuration; absorption; processing;
              squeezing; termination;
              error recovery.
            - reset_recovery_result [coverage target; failure observation]: no stale response; stale response
              observed; next valid session
              passes; next valid session fails.

            Cross:
            - reset_phase_x_pending_response_kind_x_recovery_result.
            - Keep stale or phase-inconsistent pending responses visible to the reset checker.
            '''
    }
    {
      name: state_write_ownership_cg
      desc: '''
            STATE_WRITE ownership and claim-lifecycle coverage. Keep claim state, engine phase,
            block ownership, commands, and app-collision outcomes together.

            Coverage requirements (classification in brackets):
            - state_write_claim [coverage target]: unclaimed; claimed; claim denied.
            - engine_idle [coverage target]: idle; absorb; squeeze/processing.
            - claim_lifecycle_event [coverage target]: claim accepted; CONTINUE release; DONE release; reset
              release.
            - claim_phase [coverage target]: claim inactive; claimed; released.
            - block_owner [coverage target]: software; application interface; no active owner.
            - command_outcome [coverage target]: STATE_WRITE accepted; rejected while busy;
              rejected by sideload
              restriction; CONTINUE; DONE.
            - state_model_phase [coverage target]: claim inactive; claim active and idle; resumed absorb; done/cleared;
              reset.
            - claim_attempt [coverage target]: STATE_WRITE request under each owner/engine phase.
            - claim_result [coverage target]: accepted; denied while busy; denied during
              app ownership.
            - app_id [coverage target]: dynamic OTBN; each static application interface.
            - collision_timing [coverage target]: same-cycle; adjacent-cycle; non-overlapping.
            - state_share [coverage target]: share0; share1.

            Crosses:
            - claim_x_engine_idle_x_share; claim_phase_x_command;
              block_owner_x_claim_attempt_x_outcome;
              app_id_x_collision_timing_x_claim_result.
            '''
    }
    {
      name: state_address_decode_cg
      desc: '''
            STATE aperture address-decode and share-selection coverage. Separate valid word,
            boundary, and invalid-selector behavior from ownership and data-pattern coverage.

            Coverage requirements (classification in brackets):
            - state_share [coverage target]: share0; share1.
            - claim_phase [coverage target]: claim inactive; claimed; released.
            - address_boundary [coverage target]: first/last valid selector; gap boundary; aperture end.
            - valid_word_index_class [coverage target]: first word; middle word; last valid word; other
              valid word.
            - word_index [coverage target]: first; middle; last valid; other valid word.
            - invalid_address_class [coverage target]: share0/share1 gap; share1 tail; boundary-adjacent
              invalid.
            - address_validity [coverage target]: valid; invalid gap; invalid tail; adjacent boundary.
            - mask_class [coverage target]: full word; single-byte partial; multi-byte partial; zero mask
              if legal.
            - access_type [coverage target]: read; full-word write; partial-mask write.
            - address_result [coverage target; failure observation]: valid selector accepted; invalid selector
              returns zero; invalid write
              dropped; alias/wrap detected.
            - state_write_claim [coverage target]: unclaimed; claimed; claim denied.
            - masking_mode [coverage target]: masking enabled; masking disabled.
            - byte_mask_class [coverage target]: full word; single-byte partial; multi-byte partial; zero mask if
              legal.

            Crosses:
            - share_x_address_boundary;
              address_class_x_access_type_x_result;
              masking_x_claim_x_share_x_address_validity_x_mask_class.
            '''
    }
    {
      name: state_write_data_bus_cg
      desc: '''
            STATE write-data, bus-attribute, endianness, and sequence coverage. Keep write patterns
            and write acceptance outcomes with their byte-mask and share crosses.

            Coverage requirements (classification in brackets):
            - write_data_pattern [coverage target]: all zero; all one; alternating; walking one; walking
              zero;
              address-derived; distinct-share pattern.
            - byte_mask_class [coverage target]: full word; single-byte partial; multi-byte partial; zero
              mask if
              legal.
            - write_acceptance_outcome [coverage target]: accepted; acknowledged-but-dropped;
              TL attribute error;
              invalid address.
            - tl_error [coverage target]: no TL error; adapter partial-write error.
            - state_endianness [coverage target]: little-endian; byte-swapped/big-endian.
            - masking_mode [coverage target]: masking enabled; masking disabled.
            - valid_word_index_class [coverage target]: first word; middle word; last valid word; other
              valid word.
            - write_sequence_kind [coverage target]: single write; repeated overwrite; full state sweep.
            - state_share [coverage target]: share0; share1.
            - state_write_claim [coverage target]: unclaimed; claimed; claim denied.
            - claim_phase [coverage target]: claim inactive; claim active; released.
            - configuration_class [coverage target]: legal supported configuration; unsupported/invalid
              configuration.
            - required_feature_bins [coverage target]: each build configuration and required feature bin.

            Crosses:
            - share_x_word_index_x_data_pattern; claim_x_byte_mask_x_tl_error;
              byte_mask_x_write_outcome; endianness_x_share_x_pattern;
              write_sequence_x_claim_phase_x_share; masking_x_endianness_x_share;
              configuration_x_required_feature_bins.
            '''
    }
    {
      name: state_read_semantics_cg
      desc: '''
            STATE read-data and masking-semantics coverage, isolated from STATE write behavior.

            Coverage requirements (classification in brackets):
            - read_share_result [coverage target]: share0 data; share1 data; share1 forced
              zero when unmasked;
              invalid/gap behavior.
            - state_share [coverage target]: share0; share1.
            - masking_mode [coverage target]: masking enabled; masking disabled.

            Cross:
            - masking_x_share_x_read_result.
            '''
    }
    {
      name: state_write_error_recovery_cg
      desc: '''
            STATE_WRITE error-handling and recovery coverage, separated from ownership, address,
            and data-path coverage. Cover expected KMAC command/security errors and TL adapter
            partial-write errors distinctly.

            Coverage requirements (classification in brackets):
            - configuration_class [coverage target]: legal configuration; unsupported/invalid configuration.
            - command_outcome [coverage target]: STATE_WRITE accepted; rejected while busy;
              rejected by sideload
              restriction; CONTINUE; DONE.
            - error_outcome [coverage target]: none; expected command error; TL partial-write
              error; sideload
              restriction; entropy-not-ready; app collision; fatal escalation.
            - state_write_claim [coverage target]: unclaimed; claimed; claim denied.
            - state_share [coverage target]: share0; share1.
            - lc_escalation_phase [coverage target]: before claim; during claimed write; coincident
              with write; after
              release.
            - lc_escalation_alert [coverage target; failure observation]: no alert; expected alert;
              missing/unexpected alert.

            Crosses:
            - command_x_configuration_x_error_outcome;
              lc_escalation_phase_x_claim_x_share_x_alert.
            '''
    }
    {
      name: state_write_reset_recovery_cg
      desc: '''
            STATE_WRITE reset-recovery coverage, separated from ownership and write-data coverage.
            Sample resets before/during/after claimed share writes and check recovery of the
            share-write state.

            Coverage requirements (classification in brackets):
            - reset_phase [coverage target]: before claim; after claim; between share writes; after
              final write; after
              release.
            - share_write_phase [coverage target]: no share written; share0 partially/fully written; share1
              partially/fully written; all intended shares written.
            - recovery_result [coverage target; failure observation]: claim/write-enable cleared; stale
              claim/write-enable remains.

            Cross:
            - reset_phase_x_share_write_phase_x_recovery.
            '''
    }
    {
      name: save_restore_mode_alignment_cg
      desc: '''
            Save/restore operation mode, rate, checkpoint position, and STOP-alignment coverage.
            Error/recovery and reset/recovery coverage remain in their dedicated groups.

            Coverage requirements (classification in brackets):
            - hash_mode [coverage target]: SHA3; SHAKE; cSHAKE; KMAC.
            - keccak_strength [coverage target]: L128; L224; L256; L384; L512 as supported by mode.
            - keccak_rate_bytes [coverage target]: 72; 104; 136; 144; 168.
            - save_position_blocks [coverage target]: after 1 full block; after 2 blocks; repeated save
              after later
              blocks.
            - stop_alignment [coverage target]: aligned; non-aligned.
            - post_restore_tail_length [coverage target]: 0; 1; rate-1; representative partial tail.

            Crosses:
            - hash_mode_x_strength_x_rate; hash_mode_x_strength_x_save_position;
              mode_x_rate_x_alignment; mode_x_tail_length; save_alignment_x_tail_length.
            '''
    }
    {
      name: save_restore_context_switch_cg
      desc: '''
            Save/restore command sequencing, ownership switching, and repeated/interleaved
            checkpoint coverage.

            Coverage requirements (classification in brackets):
            - context_switch_command [coverage target]: STOP; STATE_WRITE; CONTINUE; DONE.
            - context_phase [coverage target]: absorbing; stopped; state-write claimed; continued; done/released.
            - interleaved_context_count [coverage target]: 1 context; 2 contexts.
            - save_restore_iteration_count [coverage target]: single save; repeated saves (2+).
            - state_write_claim_result [coverage target]: claim accepted; rejected while
              busy; rejected during app
              ownership.
            - application_activity [coverage target]: no app active; app active; same-cycle app/STATE_WRITE
              collision.
            - stream_id [coverage target]: first or second interleaved message stream.
            - save_order [coverage target]: first or later checkpoint saved.
            - restore_order [coverage target]: matching checkpoint restored; out-of-order restore.
            - checkpoint_age [coverage target]: newest; older retained checkpoint.

            Crosses:
            - command_transition_sequence;
              save_order_x_restore_order_x_stream_id;
              save_count_x_checkpoint_age;
              app_activity_x_state_write_claim_result.
            '''
    }
    {
      name: save_restore_state_integrity_cg
      desc: '''
            Save/restore share data, readback, metadata, and digest-integrity coverage. Preserve
            diagnostic mismatches and sample required feature bins in each build configuration.

            Coverage requirements (classification in brackets):
            - state_share [coverage target]: share0; share1.
            - state_word_boundary [coverage target]: first; middle; last valid word.
            - state_share_readback [coverage target]: share0 preserved; share1 preserved;
              share1 zero when unmasked.
            - masking_mode [coverage target]: enabled; disabled.
            - context_data_pattern [coverage target]: distinct share patterns; all-zero/all-one; asymmetric/walking
              patterns.
            - digest_compare_result [coverage target; failure observation]: match uninterrupted reference;
              mismatch diagnostic.
            - access_type [coverage target]: claimed-state read; save read; restore write.
            - metadata_field [coverage target]: saved/restored metadata field.
            - metadata_roundtrip_result [coverage target; failure observation]: preserved; mismatch.
            - modified_share_region [coverage target]: first; middle; last word region.
            - modified_share_digest_effect [coverage target; failure observation]: expected diagnostic mismatch;
              no effect detected.
            - build_config [coverage target]: each supported masked/unmasked build.
            - required_bins [coverage target]: each required feature bin for the build.

            Crosses:
            - masking_x_share_x_access; share_x_word_boundary_x_pattern;
              metadata_field_x_roundtrip_result;
              modified_share_x_word_region_x_digest_effect;
              build_config_x_required_bins. Sample every build configuration and required feature
              bin.
            '''
    }
    {
      name: save_restore_error_recovery_cg
      desc: '''
            Save/restore error-handling and recovery coverage, separated from normal configuration,
            alignment, and context-switch coverage. Cover app-collision, entropy-readiness,
            sideload, and configuration-restore errors together with their recovery outcomes.

            Coverage requirements (classification in brackets):
            - save_restore_error [coverage target]: none; ErrSwStopNotBlockAligned;
              ErrSwIssuedCmdInAppActive;
              ErrSwHashingWithoutEntropyReady; sideload restriction error.
            - entropy_ready_at_continue [coverage target]: ready; not ready.
            - key_source [coverage target]: software key; sideload key.
            - sideload_context_switch_attempt [coverage target]: sideload disabled; sideload enabled
              with command
              rejected.
            - configuration_restored [coverage target; failure observation]: original mode/strength/endianness
              restored; mismatch/negative
              test.
            - masking_mode [coverage target]: enabled; disabled.
            - continue_outcome [coverage target]: CONTINUE accepted; blocked by
              entropy-not-ready; blocked by other
              error.
            - context_switch_command [coverage target]: STOP; STATE_WRITE; CONTINUE; DONE.
            - context_command_outcome [coverage target]: accepted; rejected by sideload
              restriction; rejected by
              another save/restore error.
            - config_changed [coverage target]: configuration changed after save; original configuration restored;
              restore mismatch.
            - sideload [coverage target]: disabled; enabled.

            Crosses:
            - masking_x_entropy_ready_x_continue_outcome;
              sideload_x_context_command_x_outcome;
              config_changed_x_config_restored_x_digest_result.
            '''
    }
    {
      name: save_restore_reset_recovery_cg
      desc: '''
            Save/restore reset-recovery coverage, separated from normal configuration and
            context-switch coverage. Cover reset points in the claim/share-loading/CONTINUE
            lifecycle and whether a fresh operation succeeds after reset.

            Coverage requirements (classification in brackets):
            - reset_phase [coverage target]: before claim; after claim; between share writes; after
              state load; before
              CONTINUE.
            - share_write_phase [coverage target]: no share written; share0 partially/fully written; share1
              partially/fully written; all intended shares loaded.
            - fresh_operation_result [coverage target; failure observation]: no stale response; fresh operation
              passes; fresh operation
              fails.

            Cross:
            - reset_phase_x_share_write_phase_x_fresh_operation_result.
            '''
    }
  ]
}
